    "product_id": "6363f564-ff17-49d7-96fe-f86735186b0f",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 061911\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1211 PM MST Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures with minimal day-to-day changes will \n  carry through the rest of this week as lower deserts top out \n  around 97 to 108 degrees.\n\n- An Extreme Heat Warning is in effect for portions of southeast \n  California and southwest Arizona from Wednesday through Friday \n  as temperatures warm to near record levels, resulting in Major \n  HeatRisk.\n\n- Dry weather will persist through the at least the end of the \n  workweek before a potential cooler and more unsettled pattern \n  arrives this weekend into early next week.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nRAP analysis this afternoon shows a mostly quiet conditions \nacross much of the western CONUS, with only a very weak trough \npresent over the Intermountain West. Outside of this minor \ndisturbance, high pressure persists over the region and will \ncontinue to do so through the middle portion of the week. 500mb \nheights currently stand around 588-591 dam, nothing too out of the\nordinary for the Desert Southwest, but still 1-1.5 standard \ndeviations from typical climatological normals during the front \nhalf of October. This relatively enhanced atmospheric thickness \nwill allow for temperatures to warm further through the middle \nportion of the week, pushing readings to around 10 degrees or so \nabove normal for this time of year, which translates to lower \ndesert highs between 97-107 degrees. The higher end of the range \nwill be collocated with the highest heights, which will be focused \nover southwestern Arizona and southeastern California. Although \nexpected temperatures will be far from what we see during the peak\nof summer, they will still reach near record levels (see climate \nsection), which will produce areas of Major HeatRisk. As a result,\nthe existing Extreme Heat Watch has been upgraded to a Warning, \nwhich will go into effect starting late Wednesday morning.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nThough ridging aloft will begin to weaken during the back half of\nthe week, low-lvl thermal profiles will lag behind a bit as 850mb\ncontinue to rise toward 26-27C into Thursday. This will result in\nsfc highs reaching 102-108 degrees across the western deserts, \nagain nearing daily records in Yuma and the Imperial Valley. Major\nHeatRisk is expected to be most expansive on Thursday afternoon, \nleading to a spatial extension of the Extreme Heat Warning to \ninclude the entire Lower Colorado River Valley and a portion of \nthe Gila River Valley in Yuma County including Dateland and Tacna.\nPortions of south-central AZ will also see highs climb to around \n100-103 or around 10 degrees above normal on Thursday, although \nthe HeatRisk is expected to remain in the Moderate category. \nFriday will be a transition day where a deepening trough over the \nPac NW which in turn induces SW flow aloft over the West Coast. \nAlthough we will begin to see a reduction in overall hghts aloft \non Friday afternoon, low-lvl thermal profiles will be slow to \nrespond as 850 mb temperatures are still expected to reach 25-26C \nacross the much of the forecast area equating to sfc highs around \n100-105 degrees in most lower desert communities. Due to these\ntemperatures and lingering Major HeatRisk for the Imperial Valley\nand Yuma area, the Extreme Heat Warning will continue through\nFriday.\n\nWe then turn our attention to an upcoming pattern shift heading \ninto this weekend as most deterministic models and ensemble \nmembers are trending toward an overall troffing pattern setting up\nacross the western CONUS. There is now better model agreement \nthat a stronger and deeper trough will transition into a cutoff \nlow over the Pac NW on Saturday and gradually sink southward into \nCA Sunday and Monday. Meanwhile, latest forecast guidance from the\nNHC shows an eventual turn to the northeast by Rachel this \nweekend with the remnants of the storm itself being pulled \nnortheastward and phasing with the west coast trough. As this \npattern transpires, there should be an increase in southwesterly \nflow aloft over the Desert Southwest which will begin to pull \nPacific moisture into the region by late Saturday into Sunday. \nCoincidentally, rain chances will return to the much of the region\nwith NBM PoPs now around 20-50% across south-central AZ on Sunday\nand even up to 40-60% across the foothills NE of Phoenix by \nMonday. There is still high uncertainty in rainfall amounts at \nthis time but there is at least a chance of some higher totals \nexceeding 1.50\" across AZ high terrain. As 500 mb hghts begin to \nlower ahead of the approaching trough along with increasing cloud \ncover and moisture, high temperatures will take a dive from the \nmid 90s on Saturday to the low to mid 80s by Monday.\n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1720Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo aviation weather concerns under FEW occasional mid-level cloud\ndecks can be expected throughout the TAF period. Winds today will\nreturn to a more traditional diurnal pattern with the westerly\nshift likely by mid-late afternoon. Wind speeds are expected to\nremain light, mostly below 10 kts. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather concerns will exist through the next 24 hours\nunder mostly clear skies. Winds will generally remain light, with\nextended periods of variability and directions favoring a westerly\ncomponent at times.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions with well above normal temperatures will continue \nthrough the week, with afternoon highs reaching up to 100-107 \ndegrees across the lower deserts (peaking Thursday). Winds will \nreturn to their more typical light diurnal tendencies today and\npersist into Thursday before increasing again Friday into this\nweekend. MinRHs will mostly range from 15-25% across the western \ndistricts to 20-35% across eastern districts through the week with\nfair to good overnight recovery (35-60%) area-wide. Humidities \nwill be lowest on Thursday when temperatures are likely at their \nhottest, but winds will still be light at this time, so there are \nno elevated fire weather concerns. No wetting rain expected \nthrough at least Saturday. However, chances for wetting rainfall\nis expected to arrive by the end of the weekend/start of next \nweek as a weather system begins to move into the region.\n\n&&\n\n.Climate...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate Yuma     El Centro\n----     ----   ---------\nOct 7    111 in 2024105 in 1991\nOct 8 107 in 1996    106 in 1996\nOct 9 106 in 2024104 in 1996\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM Wednesday to 8 PM MST Friday for \n     AZZ532.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM to 8 PM MST Thursday for AZZ530-\n     536.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM Wednesday to 8 PM PDT Friday for \n     CAZ566-567.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM to 8 PM PDT Thursday for CAZ569-\n     570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...RW\nLONG TERM...Salerno/RW \nAVIATION...Whittock/Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict/Salerno\nClimate... RW\n",
